  "usefulness_limitations": {
    "usefulness": [
      {
        "use_case": "Grading programming labs at scale (CS1/CS2 courses)",
        "example": "300 students submit 'sort n integers'. The agent grades all of them overnight under identical limits. A student whose bubble sort times out on n = 100000 receives the failing input size, the measured O(n^2) growth curve and the hottest loop line, instead of a bare 'time limit exceeded'.",
        "benefit": "Consistent, explainable grades in minutes; teaching assistants only look at flagged or borderline submissions."
      },
      {
        "use_case": "Coding-interview and hiring screens",
        "example": "A company runs 500 candidate submissions for an 'add two integers safely' task. The agent ranks them by correctness, overflow handling and memory safety, and every rejection is backed by a concrete failing input such as '2147483647 1'.",
        "benefit": "Fast first-round filtering where every decision can be justified metric by metric."
      },
      {
        "use_case": "Plagiarism and LLM-copy triage",
        "example": "Two submissions reach a similarity of 0.93 (threshold 0.85) even though every variable was renamed and every comment rewritten. The agent flags the pair with the matching line ranges, so the instructor inspects 2 files instead of 300. A third submission matches a solution generated by an LLM for the same statement and is flagged as a suspected LLM copy.",
        "benefit": "Review time goes only to suspicious cases, and the evidence (lines, similarity) is already prepared."
      },
      {
        "use_case": "Self-check before the deadline / continuous integration for assignments",
        "example": "A student's qsort solution passes the visible sample, but the agent's generated boundary case '2 / 0 -2147483648' shows that the comparator 'return a - b' overflows. The minimized counterexample and the suggested fix arrive before the student submits.",
        "benefit": "Finds bugs that fixed test suites miss and teaches with the smallest input that breaks the program."
      }
    ],
    "limitations": [
      {
        "limitation": "Testing cannot prove correctness",
        "example": "add_magic_value.c prints 0 whenever the first number is 4242. It passes 10/10 hidden tests and all 18 generated edge cases and is graded 97.9/100, because neither the tests nor the generators happen to produce 4242.",
        "mitigation": "Larger random budgets and instructor-specified 'must-test' values reduce the risk but never remove it; suspicious special-casing still needs a human reader."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "Complexity is estimated from measured operation counts, not proven",
        "example": "sort_memmove_insertion.c finds each insertion point by binary search and shifts with memmove(). Its O(n^2) work happens inside libc, which gcov does not instrument, so the executed-line count grows like n log n, the agent reports 'O(n log n) confirmed' and grades it 99.8/100. Likewise a quicksort with a first-element pivot is O(n log n) on the random inputs used for measuring, but O(n^2) on sorted input.",
        "mitigation": "Include adversarial (sorted) inputs and a large hidden test that only an efficient solution passes within the time limit."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "Similarity-based copy detection has false positives and false negatives",
        "example": "For 'add two integers', two honest solutions are almost the same token stream; the agent avoids flagging them only because of reference-code subtraction and the minimum-shared-fingerprints rule. Conversely, a student who takes an LLM answer and rewrites loops (for -> while), splits functions and reorders statements drops below 0.85 and is not flagged. When a pair is flagged, both students are flagged: the agent cannot tell who copied whom.",
        "mitigation": "Flags are review signals, never automatic verdicts; the instructor decides after reading the matched lines."
      },
      {
        "limitation": "Verdicts depend on the platform and on external models",
        "example": "On Windows (MinGW) `long` is 32 bits, so a solution that sums into `long` overflows and fails tests that pass on Linux; ASan is unavailable there, so memory_safety falls back to UBSan trap mode and a leaking program loses no points. The LLM readability score can also change after `ollama pull` updates the model weights, and a program running at 0.95-1.05 s CPU can flip between AC and TLE across machines.",
        "mitigation": "Run official grading in the provided Linux/Docker image, keep the LLM response cache with the course records, and keep the hidden tests far from the time limit; every report records tool versions and hashes."
      }
    ]
  },
